CLAIMS=[
('BLS Consumer Expenditure Survey: the average household spends $78,535 a year ($6,545/month); housing $26,266; transportation $13,318; food $10,164.','the average American household spends $78,535 per year',P+'BLSCE24: "Consumer Expenditures--2024" (released 2025-12-19, before the 2026-03-27 upload): average annual expenditures $78,535; housing $26,266; transportation $13,318; food $10,169 (stated $10,164, off by $5). '+C+'78,535 / 12 = $6,545; 26,266 / 12 = $2,189. Verified. Note these are per consumer unit, average income before taxes $104,207.',['BLSCE24']),
('For the first time in American history we spend more on food away from home than on groceries.','we now spend more on food away from home than on groceries',P+'BLSCE24 shows the opposite in the same survey the video cites: food at home $6,224 vs food away from home $3,945 per consumer unit in 2024. '+R+'USDA ERS national food-expenditure series has shown food-away-from-home above food-at-home since around 2010, so "first time" is not recent. Misleading as stated.',['BLSCE24']),
('Average new car payment $772; one in five pay over $1,000; average new car price a record $50,326; one in five new loans is 84 months.','The average new car payment in the United States right now is $772 a month.',SN+'ED4Q: Edmunds Q4 2025 average payment $772 and more than 1 in 5 at $1,000+ (search summaries, NICK_264 session). KBBDEC: Cox/KBB December 2025 ATP $50,326 (record for December). The 84-month share not checked.',['ED4Q','KBBDEC']),
('Average new car cost $20,356 in 2000 vs $50,326 today: +147% against 88% overall inflation.','The average new car cost $20,356 in 2000.',C+'50,326 / 20,356 - 1 = 147.2%, reproduces. '+P+'FREDCPI: CPI-U 2000 annual average 172.2 to September 2025 324.245 = +88.3%; the inflation figure is verified. The $20,356 base not checked.',['FREDCPI']),
('AAA 2025: total vehicle ownership costs $965 a month.','comes out to $965 a month',R+'AAA Your Driving Costs 2025 (~$11,577 a year for a new vehicle driven 15,000 miles, ~$965/month) consistent by recall; not fetched. Includes depreciation on a new car.',[]),
('Delivery users spend $130-152/month, 36% fees; subscriptions guessed $86 vs actual $219 (2.5x); phone and internet $280/month vs ~$60 in 2000.','You think you\'re paying $86, you\'re paying $219.',C+'219 / 86 = 2.55x ("two and a half") reproduces. '+R+'The $86/$219 figures match a 2022 C+R Research survey (dated). The $280 connectivity and delivery figures have no stated source. '+U,[]),
('Childcare $10,600-15,570 per child per year; infant care $24,000 in Massachusetts and California; 43 million student borrowers averaging $39,075; payments $200-503.','averages $10,600 to $15,570 per child per year',R+'Child Care Aware 2024 national price ranges and ED federal portfolio (~43 million borrowers, ~$39,000 average) consistent by recall; not fetched. The PDF-verified NY Fed total in NICK_260 is $1.66T (different denominator). '+U,[]),
('The Garcias ($90,000, ~$6,000 take-home): itemised costs $4,333; after $380 student loans fixed costs $5,093; $907 left; $25,000 emergency fund takes over 2 years; 15% retirement is $1,125; "$275 of breathing room".','After student loans, they\'re at $5,093 in fixed expenses',C+'The ten items sum to $4,333 (reproduces). But $4,333 + $380 = $4,713, not $5,093, leaving $1,287, not $907 (the script adds $760). 25,000 / 907 = 27.6 months ("over 2 years") and 15% x 90,000 / 12 = $1,125 reproduce on its own numbers. The "$275" margin is never derived and contradicts $907. Internally inconsistent.',[]),
('40% of $100,000 households live paycheck to paycheck; Goldman Sachs found 40% of $500,000 households feel the same.','Goldman Sachs found that 40% of households earning $500,000',R+'PYMNTS/LendingClub surveys report ~40%+ of $100K+ earners paycheck to paycheck (consistent). The Goldman figure resembles a 2023 Goldman Sachs Ayco survey of corporate employees (about a third of those earning $300K+ reported paycheck-to-paycheck), not households at $500K; likely misattributed. Not fetched. '+U,[]),
('At ~10%, $772/month for 30 years = $1.73M (half = $865,000); $219 = $495,000; $280 = $633,000; $1,500-2,000 = $3.4-4.5M; $30 = $67,000; $40 = $90,000.','you\'d have $1.73 million dollars',C+'Monthly compounding at 10%/12: $1,745,097 (~$1.73M; annual compounding gives $1.52M); $386 = $872,548; $219 = $495,047; $280 = $632,937; $1,500 = $3.39M; $2,000 = $4.52M; $30 = $67,815; $40 = $90,420. All reproduce; nominal, pre-tax, before fees.',[]),
('Average indebted household owes $11,400 on cards at just under 21% APR; minimum payments generate over $18,000 of interest.','owes $11,400 on their credit cards',C+'At 20.9% with a minimum of interest + 1% (floor $25), $11,400 costs $18,815 in interest over ~26 years, reproducing "more than $18,000" (a 3% minimum gives $15,050). '+R+'The $11,400 balance and Bankrate groceries/utilities/childcare driver not fetched. '+U,[]),
('The normal portfolio generates a savings rate of about 3.6 cents per dollar.','a savings rate of about 3.6 cents per dollar earned',P+'FREDSAV: BEA personal saving rate, current vintage: 2025-12 5.0%, 2026-01 5.6%, 2026-02 5.3%. 3.6% does not match the current vintage; the figure available at upload (earlier vintage) was not checked, so the claim is not confirmed.',['FREDSAV']),
('Nearly 40% of Americans have overspent to impress others; millionaires drive 3-year-old cars.','Nearly 40% of Americans have overspent to impress someone else.',R+'Consistent with a 2024 Bankrate/CNBC-type survey and Stanley-style millionaire research by recall; not fetched. '+U,[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "The Shocking Cost of Being \'Normal\' in 2026" frames average spending as a hidden cost. The script walks through BLS spending categories, a household budget and the 30-year opportunity cost. WEAK against the 10-peer cohort (0.485x, just under the 0.5 threshold) but TYPICAL on the 5-peer window (0.808x), so the tier is unstable. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background. Left: the recurring cartoon man (grey T-shirt) with hands on cheeks, mouth open in alarm, holding a long receipt labelled "INVOICE" with garbled line items. Right: a heavy black headline "$5,000 A MONTH?" with a curved arrow pointing at the receipt. At 320x180 the headline and receipt stay legible; line items do not. The $5,000 figure does not appear in the script (which uses $6,545 average and the Garcias\' $5,093).'
SCRIPT='A cost-of-living essay: Target checkout hook; "normal is a trap"; BLS CE total and categories (housing, transportation, food); car payments and prices, AAA ownership cost; food away from home and delivery fees; subscriptions; phone and internet; childcare; student loans; the Garcias budget; paycheck-to-paycheck at $100K and $500K; 30-year opportunity cost of the car, subscriptions and connectivity; why (car prices vs CPI, loan terms, subscription price creep, visibility trap); credit card debt; three actions (find your number, cut invisible overhead, spending gap); small-change compounding; close. Directly verified: BLS CE 2024 totals and categories (food $10,169 vs stated $10,164), CPI +88% since 2000. Contradicted by the same survey: "more on food away than groceries". Internally inconsistent: Garcias fixed costs ($4,713, not $5,093) and the "$275" margin. Saving rate 3.6% not supported by current-vintage data. Opportunity-cost and card-interest arithmetic reproduce. The stored ending is complete.'
BEATS=['Hook: Target checkout.','Normal as a slow leak.','When did you choose these costs?','Disclaimer; BLS CE total.','Housing and rent.','Transportation; car payment and price; AAA.','Food; delivery fees.','Subscriptions.','Phone and internet.','Childcare; student loans.','The Garcias budget.','What thin margins feel like; raises and lifestyle creep.','Paycheck to paycheck at $100K and $500K.','30-year opportunity cost.','Not anti-car; the monthly framing.','Why: car prices, loan terms, subscriptions, visibility.','Credit card debt.','Three actions.','Small-change compounding; close.']
